QUESTION=Identify Knickerbocker and explain the framing function for Irving's tale.
RESULT=Diedrich Knickerbocker is Washington Irving's fictional/pseudonymous Dutch-American historian persona, used earlier for A History of New York and reused as the supposed source of Sleepy Hollow. The frame presents the tale as found/collected history or folklore, creating playful historical distance and allowing tension between factual history, legend, and storytelling.
SOURCE_DOOR_1=Institutional archive | New York Public Library Washington Irving papers | https://archives.nypl.org/mss/1524
SOURCE_DOOR_2=Reference/biographical corroboration | Washington Irving reference results identify Diedrich Knickerbocker as a pseudonym/persona and A History of New York as the earlier use.
SOURCE_DOOR_3=Literary analysis | LitCharts Diedrich Knickerbocker analysis | https://www.litcharts.com/lit/the-legend-of-sleepy-hollow/characters/diedrich-knickerbocker
SOURCE_DOOR_4=Literary analysis | SparkNotes Diedrich Knickerbocker analysis | https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/rip-van-winkle/character-diedrich-knickerbocker/
EVIDENCE_NOTE=Institutional evidence establishes Irving's use of the pseudonym/persona; literary-analysis doors converge that the framing device imitates collected history/folklore and plays with truth/authenticity. Interpretive claims remain interpretation rather than hard biographical fact.
REUSABLE_SEARCH_FUNCTION_CANDIDATE=PSEUDONYM_FRAME_SOURCE_CHAIN: when a work attributes itself to a named narrator/editor/source, test whether the identity is real, pseudonymous, fictional, or composite; trace earlier uses; verify creator linkage through institutional/primary evidence; then compare independent interpretation of the frame's function while preserving fact-vs-interpretation boundary.
